    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 132038\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1238 PM PST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Unsettled weather returns to the region through tomorrow morning. \n  Wind Advisories are in effect for much of the Lower Great Basin \n  through tonight. \n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Thursday\n\nToday may be the warmest days for the rest of the year as a much \ncooler and unsettled weather pattern sets up and persists into the \nend of the month. Highs peak in the 70s for desert valleys today, \nthen slowly trend downward into the 60s by next week. Current \nsatellite imagery shows mid and high level clouds across the region \nin the southerly flow regime ahead of the main trough axis and front \npushing into Central and Northern California. LAS ACARS Sounding \nshows a small cap at 700mb preventing upscale growth of clouds early \nthis afternoon; however expect this cap to break toward sunset \nallowing for scattered shower development in Mohave and Lincoln \ncounties. A few showers possible around the Las Vegas valley but \nanything more than a trace locally will be fairly isolated. Greatest \nimpacts from precipitation will be confined to the Sierra where \nstrong orographic lifting and a 120 kt WNW jet streak will promote \nheavy snow at higher elevations. A Winter Storm Warning is in effect \nfor elevations above 7,000 feet in the Eastern Sierra for Friday \ninto Saturday afternoon. Around a foot of new snow will be possible \nin the Sierra Crest, and 5 to 10 inches of snow expected between \n7,000 ft and 9,500 ft. Downsloping/rain shadowing from the Sierra \nwill significantly limit rainfall in the Lower Great Basin and \nMohave Desert tonight as the trough axis swings through the region. \nThe best chance for showers outside of the Sierra will be across Nye \nand Lincoln counties and higher elevations of San Bernardino and \nClark Counties tonight shifting into Mohave County Saturday morning.\n\nWinds ramp up today with high probabilities for frequent gusts over \n40 mph for Nye, Esmeralda, Inyo, NW San Bernardino County, and far \nNW Clark County. Wind Advisories are already in effect through \ntonight for 20 to 30 mph sustained southerly winds and gusts to 40 \nto 50 mph. The Advisory does not include Las Vegas however wind \ngusts around 30 mph will still be possible. Winds across the region \nease before rain fully exits the area Saturday.\n\nA brief period of calm and dry conditions set up for the latter half \nof Saturday into Monday morning as ridging builds in overhead ahead \nof the next system.\n\nAnother, potentially stronger, system approaches the region Monday \nbringing more widespread rainfall chances and cooler air to the \nDesert Southwest. Guidance today indicates a West Coast trough \ndiving much deeper than the one this weekend, allowing for better \nmoisture advection northward. Precipitable water values may near \n1.00\" around Las Vegas Monday and latest ECMWF ensembles show strong \nagreement in measurable rainfall through the Mohave Desert. Rain \nlooks to become widespread into Monday afternoon for the areas east \nof the Sierra Nevada, but rain rates look to stay light to moderate. \nRainfall totals from Monday into Tuesday look modest (0.10 to 0.25\") \nfor all but Northeast Clark Co, Mohave Co, and Lincoln Co which will \nrange from 0.30 to 1.0\" after showers exit to the east. Currently, \nsnow in the upper elevations of the Sierra from this next system \nlooks to stay relatively light as the best forcing and moisture \nadvection remain south and east. Additional chances for rain \nexpected later in the week as another system will likely be on the \nheals of the departing one. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty \nsoutheasterly winds develop late this morning, intensifying and \nturning to the southwest this afternoon with peak gusts between 20 \nand 25 knots. These winds should become less gusty but remain over \n10 knots in the evening, then strengthen again in the early morning \nhours. Although precipitation is not expected within the vicinity of \nthe terminal, showers may develop over high terrain around the Las \nVegas Valley, which could also lead to terrain obscuration. SCT to \nBKN clouds at or above 10kft persist throughout the forecast\nperiod.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Expect widespread gusty \nwinds this afternoon and tonight. Some of the strongest winds will \nbe in the Owens Valley including KBIH, where southeasterly gusts \nwill peak between 30 and 35 knots this afternoon. These winds will \nshift to the north overnight and a few showers within the vicinity \nof the terminal cannot be ruled out. Elsewhere, gusts peak out of \nthe southwest between 20 and 25 knots in the Las Vegas Valley and \nColorado River Valley, and out of the west between 30 and 35 knots \nat KDAG. Clouds will be SCT to BKN around 10kft but lower bases may \noccur with any showers that develop. Terrain obscuration is expected \nwith rain and snow showers over area mountains.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
